  "result": "The 30-minute lesson on O3 is written, with a short quiz and a cheat sheet. I haven't opened the lesson in a browser or run any checker on it. I also didn't test the external links, though each one came from this session's searches or fetched pages.\n\n- **Lesson:** `lessons/0001-context-and-memory.html`. It covers four ideas, each with an example and a practice check:\n  - what the context window holds and why the model is stateless;\n  - how the context and cost grow between steps, including context rot and trimming;\n  - why nothing carries between sessions, and how notes and a database fill the gap;\n  - retrieval over the manuals, pre-inference versus just-in-time.\n- **Lesson extras:** It also has a worked cost example, a faded practice task and a \"design it\" exercise for the makerspace assistant. I estimate it at about 3,800 words, inside the 2,700–6,750 range for a 30-minute lesson.\n- **Quiz:** `quizzes/0002.json` has five items on O3, and its key is `keys/0002.json`. None repeat the lesson's practice questions. Ask me when you want to take it.\n- **Cheat sheet:** `cheatsheets/context-and-memory.html` holds the vocabulary, the token-cost arithmetic and a \"where does it live\" table.\n- **Workspace files:** I created `RESOURCES.md`, and `NOTES.md` now records the lesson and quiz.\n- **Sources:** The primary source is Anthropic's \"Effective context engineering for AI agents\", alongside the Claude docs page on context windows.\n\nI used M3 (the model remembers earlier conversations automatically), which is already in key 0001. I also introduced M7 (a bigger window means more context is always better), which is new.\n\nI found no moderated online community for agent builders, so the lesson points you to your design instructor and the makerspace staff. `RESOURCES.md` lists this as a gap.\n\nDo you want a link to an online community added once I find a good one?",
